Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 13:51:06 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] Review round 2: base-mesh cell size, pieces by vertex
 number, parallel test

- The coverage test's cell size is the median length of the 6 base-mesh
  edges nearest each trace point, measured before the pull and the cut. A
  radius that grew with the distance to the nearest edge let half a cell
  reach 0.56-0.75 cells at a tip or a corner, and the cut's own short
  edges shrank it to 0.06-0.08 cells beside them. Each rank offers its 6
  nearest owned edges and every rank takes the same 6, ties by global
  point number.
- The fraction stays at half a cell, now a named constant. On a 480-trace
  box grid no distribution has a gap to put it in: sound faults (slip
  within 10 % of the trace sampled a cell apart) reach 0.94 cells and
  wrong ones start at 0, the pull's distortion mixing the two; the tip
  shortfalls fall at whole sample spacings. A single chain stopping up to
  half a cell short of a tip is therefore added; add_fault's docstring
  says so, with the measured slip error.
- The chain's pieces are joined at global vertex numbers, not coordinates,
  so the coincident vertices of an earlier fault's slit stay apart.
- A parallel test: the refusals and acceptances agree on every rank with
  one message, and a hand-labelled chain across the partition is one
  piece, with one edge out two.

Suites: 431 passed, 1 skipped; the parallel test is not run here.

